<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fergana Valley</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ferghana, Farghana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fergana Valley is a fertile basin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, drained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and shared today by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. Chinese sources of the Han period knew it as Dayuan, famed for its horses. After the Arab conquest it became a province of the Islamic world that supplied soldiers to the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, among them the forebears of the Ikhshidid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fergana-valley.jpg" alt="Fergana Valley" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ValleDeFerganáTopográfico.svg by Rowanwindwhistler Translated into English by CitrusHemlock · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ValleDeFergan%C3%A1Topogr%C3%A1fico_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Fergana Valley is an intermountain depression between the Tien Shan in the north and the Alay in the south. It is about 300 km long and up to 70 km wide, covering about 22,000 square kilometres, and it crosses eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. Its fertility comes from the Naryn and the Kara Darya, which join near Namangan to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. The climate is dry and continental, with hot summers and frost in December and January. The name Fergana is of Persian origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As early as 500 BCE the western part of the valley formed part of Sogdiana, which owed allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>. In 329 BCE Alexander the Great founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, the Furthest, in the south-west of the valley, at the site of the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a>. Chinese chroniclers, drawing on the travels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> and published around 126 BCE, present the region as the country of the Dayuan, possibly descendants of Greek colonists, known for its Heavenly Horses. The Chinese waged war against the Dayuan in 104 BCE to obtain them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> later ruled the area until the 3rd century CE, when the Sassanid Empire invaded.</p><p>Fergana then passed between the Sassanid Empire, the Hephthalite Empire and, from the mid-6th century, the Turkic Khaganates. In the 8th century it was a field of rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and the expanding Muslim power, and the Abbasid victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751 led to the disengagement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 819 Ahmad ibn Asad was granted authority over the city of Fergana, a step in the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>. Genghis Khan invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and Fergana in 1219, and the valley later became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal dynasty</a> came from the valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> conquered the valley at the end of the 19th century, forming the province of Fergana in 1876 out of the former khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in the 1920s, and the three republics that share it became independent in 1991. The valley is ethnically diverse, populated largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, Tajik and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz people</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> cultivation, begun by the Soviets, is joined by grains, fruits and vegetables, stock breeding and mining of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, iron and other minerals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fergana Valley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Euthydemus, an Ionian Greek from Magnesia according to Polybius and possibly satrap of Sogdiana, overthrew Diodotus II around 230–220 BCE; his rule reached into Sogdiana and <b>Ferghana</b> beyond Alexandria Eschate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kara-Khanid realm joined two different worlds: the pastures of Semirechye and the Tian Shan, where Turkic tribes such as the Karluk, Chigil, Yaghma and Tukhsi herded horses and sheep, and the irrigated oases of Transoxiana, <b>Ferghana</b>, Shash and Kashgar, with their Persian- and Sogdian-speaking farmers, craftsmen and merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Babur was a Timurid prince from the <b>Fergana Valley</b>, descended from Timur on his father’s side and from Genghis Khan on his mother’s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Tughj, a commander of Central Asian descent from <b>Farghana</b>, became governor of Egypt in 935 and restored order after years of turmoil.</blockquote><a href="/state/ikhshidid-emirate">Ikhshidid emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Thereafter Ferghana was ruled by local dynasties that became the later Principality of <b>Ferghana</b>, still noted for its horses.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dayuan">Kingdom of Dayuan</a></li><li><blockquote>After the ancient Kingdom of Dayuan, the <b>Ferghana</b> valley was ruled by local dynasties whose kings took the title ikhshid.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Principality of Ferghana</a></li></ul></section>
